<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="UDP-β-L-arabinose biosynthesis II (from β-L-arabinose)" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-82" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Oryza sativa Japonica Group. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; L-Arabinose, the 4-epimer of D-xylose, is a monosaccharide found primarily in plants. It is mainly present in cell wall polymers such as pectic (rhamnogalacturonan I, rhamnogalacturonan II) and hemicellulosic (xylan, xyloglucan) components, as well as in the glycosyl-moieties of proteins such as arabinogalactan proteins and extensions |CITS:[10517829],[10640355]|. Although little is known of the incorporation of arabinose into these polymers or the contribution of the arabinosyl-substituents to the functions of the wall polymers, it has been demonstrated that arabinose-containing polymers are derived from UDP-L-arabinose, the activated sugar used by arabinosyltransferases |CITS:[FEINGOLD80]|. The formation of UDP-L-arabinose occurs via two routes: a) a salvage pathway leading to the formation of UDP-L-arabinose from free arabinose (this pathway), and b) a &lt;i&gt;de novo&lt;/i&gt; synthesis route involving the epimerisation of UDP-D-xylose by a UDP-D-xylose 4-epimerase (|FRAME:PWY-63|). In plants, the primary role of the sugar salvage pathways is presumed to be in the recycling of sugars derived from the turnover of macromolecules such as polysaccharides, galactolipids and proteoglycans, which have sugar components. &lt;b&gt;Key Notes on the Enzymes:&lt;/b&gt; Numerous bacterial, fungal, and mammalian sugar kinase genes have been isolated and have led to the identification of three evolutionarily distinct gene families: hexokinase, ribokinase and galactokinase families. In plants, few enzymes of the galactokinase have been described: both galactokinase and arabinokinase activities have been reported in cell-free preparations from seedlings of |FRAME:TAX-3916| |CITS:[14426659],[165743]|; additionally, the &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis thaliana ARA1&lt;/i&gt; gene encoding an arabinose kinase (its activity was detected in crude extracts) has been cloned |CITS:[DOLEZAL91]|. The formation of UDP-L-arabinose at the end of the salvage pathway is achieved by a UDP-L-arabinose pyrophosphorylase. An enzyme with such activity has been purified from pea sprouts. This enzyme (USP for &apos;UDP-sugar pyrophosphorylase&apos;) was shown to have a broad range of monosaccharide substrates |CITS:[15326166]|. &lt;b&gt;Important Note:&lt;/b&gt; In cell wall the majority of arabinose is in a furanose, not pyranose, configuration.</Description>
